// The per-channel MMIO operation model (docs/wb_dma_operation_model.md §3).
//
// Every operation the processor initiates against ONE DMA channel lives here.
// The engine-global operations -- PAUSE, interrupt routing, descriptor memory
// -- live on wb_dma_c, which owns an array of these.
//
// Why per-channel components rather than a `chan` parameter on every function:
//
//   The completion condition is a channel register, so an operation is a loop
//   over an indexed register access. Making the channel an instance rather than
//   an index keeps every access in the operation code index-free
//   (`regs.csr.read()`), which is both clearer and safer than indexing a
//   register array with a runtime value. It also matches the review decision
//   that operations are per-channel and a waking thread reads its own channel's
//   status register (§3.1.1).
//
//   The cost is that each channel binds its own top-level register group to its
//   own region -- see initialize below.
//
// TWO LAYERS, AND WHICH ONE YOU GET
//
// The operations here divide into a non-blocking CORE and a blocking layer
// built out of it:
//
//   core       configure_channel, set_auto_restart, set_software_pointer --
//              straight-line stores; and transfer_single_start,
//              transfer_list_start, stop_channel_start, check_completion --
//              arm-and-return plus a single-read poll. Nothing here ever
//              suspends, so this layer runs on a target with no scheduler at
//              all. It is what low-level firmware sees.
//
//   blocking   wait_completion, and the end-to-end operations transfer_single,
//              transfer_list and stop_channel. Each is a *_start() followed by
//              a wait, and the wait suspends on the `wake` channel below.
//              Compiled only when wb_dma_cfg_pkg::WB_DMA_HAS_BLOCKING.
//
// The blocking layer adds no device knowledge -- it adds only the loop. That
// is what makes the core trustworthy without a second regression: every
// SystemVerilog run drives the core through the blocking wrappers.
//
// The wait points are not an implementation detail to be undone later; they
// are the specification of the driver state machine this model projects to
// (§2.3). Splitting them out does not remove them -- it names the two halves
// the state machine already had.

import addr_reg_pkg::*;
import sync_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_cfg_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_regs_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_types_pkg::*;

component wb_dma_ch_c {

    // Which channel this is, in dma_req_i/dma_ack_o and INT_SRC bit numbering.
    int                 chan;

    // Build-time capabilities. An absent capability makes the corresponding
    // control bit silently ignored by hardware, so operations consult this
    // rather than assuming everything is present.
    wb_dma_ch_caps_s    caps;

    // This channel's MMIO register bank. A top-level register group: it is
    // bound to its own non-allocatable region, not nested inside the device's
    // global group.
    wb_dma_ch_regs_c    regs;

    // The in-progress guard: holds a token from the moment an end-to-end
    // operation arms this channel until its completion has been reported.
    //
    // PRESENT ON BOTH PROFILES, and that is the point. Only try_put() and
    // try_get() are used, and neither ever blocks (§21.9.1), so this costs a
    // target with no scheduler nothing but a saturating counter. `wake` below
    // is the member that needs a real one.
    //
    // WHAT IT BUYS. check_completion() reads CHn_CSR, and that read CLEARS ERR
    // and the three interrupt-source bits (wb_dma_ch_regs_c.pss Table 6). The
    // blocking layer contained that hazard inside a loop that exits holding the
    // value; a firmware caller driving *_start() and check_completion() by hand
    // does not. Two ways to get it wrong -- polling a channel nobody armed, and
    // polling on past a completion already consumed -- are both invisible
    // without a guard, because the device answers a stale read the same way it
    // answers a live one.
    //
    // A CHANNEL RATHER THAN A FLAG, for the same reason `wake` is one: PSS 3.1
    // has no mutable component attribute to hold the bit (§9.1.6), so there is
    // nowhere to latch "already reported". A depth-1 channel IS the latch.
    //
    // check_completion() take-and-restores: try_get() to claim the right to
    // read, then try_put() back only if the answer was PENDING. So the token
    // survives exactly as long as the operation does. It is not a lock -- the
    // one-operation-per-channel rule is still the caller's to keep -- it is a
    // detector for the case where that rule has already been broken.
    //
    // stop_channel_start() is EXEMPT and claims nothing. It exists to abort a
    // transfer that is by definition already holding the token, so claiming
    // would report the exemption as a violation. That is the same exemption
    // stop_channel.pss has always documented, now visible in the code.
    channel_c<bit, 1>   inflight;

    // The wake-up hint for this channel's waiter. NOT the completion event --
    // the event is always CHn_CSR, re-read on every wake (wait_completion.pss).
    // This only says "something happened, look again".
    //
    // WHY A CHANNEL AND NOT A FLAG. A `waiting` bit plus a broadcast to
    // whoever has it set is the shape this replaced, and it fails twice: PSS
    // has no mutable component attribute to hold the bit (3.1 dropped
    // `mutable`; component attributes are set during elaboration and read
    // thereafter, §9.1.6), and flag-then-block has a lost-wakeup window -- an
    // interrupt between arming the hardware and setting the bit is dropped and
    // the waiter blocks forever. A channel has no such window: it IS the
    // registration. An interrupt that arrives before anyone waits leaves a
    // token, and the next get() returns immediately. Nothing to set, nothing
    // to order, nothing to lose.
    //
    // DEPTH 1 IS THE DESIGN, not a default taken for granted. It makes the
    // channel a coalescing binary semaphore: interrupts arriving while a token
    // is already pending are absorbed by try_put() failing, and a channel
    // nobody is waiting on accumulates at most ONE stale token. That bound is
    // what lets notify_irq() post to every channel blind, without asking who
    // is waiting -- the worst a non-waiter suffers is one extra CSR read at
    // the top of its next operation.
    //
    // channel_c is target-only: every function on it is a `target function`,
    // so nothing here is reachable from a solve exec.
    //
    // DELIBERATELY NOT GATED, unlike everything else that exists only for the
    // blocking layer. Two reasons, one principled and one forced:
    //
    //   * A channel_c MEMBER costs a non-blocking target nothing it was not
    //     already paying. `inflight` above requires the same type, and it is
    //     get() -- not the declaration -- that needs a scheduler. Nothing on
    //     the non-blocking profile calls get(), so nothing suspends.
    //
    //   * pssparser cannot currently do it anyway. A declaration inside a
    //     `compile if` is invisible to every LATER source file (defect 4,
    //     docs/pssparser-defects-2026-08-02.md), and this member is read from
    //     blocking_ops.pss and from wb_dma_c/functions/notify_irq.pss. Gating
    //     it does not produce an error -- it produces an unresolved reference
    //     reported as `0 errors`.
    //
    // The first reason is why this is acceptable rather than merely necessary:
    // if the defect were fixed tomorrow, this member would stay ungated.
    channel_c<bit, 1>   wake;

    // Bind the bank. Called from wb_dma_c's own initialize, which is called
    // from the environment's init_down exec -- component attributes are only
    // mutable during initialization, and set_handle() is only legal from an
    // init exec.
    solve function void initialize(int id, addr_handle_t bank) {
        chan = id;
        regs.set_handle(bank);
    }
}
